{Nimzo-Indian Defense: Three Knights Variation. Both sides have what they wanted from the opening. The middlegame will be about the centre.}
1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. Nf3 b6 5. Qb3 c5 {Theory ends about here. From this point the game is decided by understanding rather than memory.} 6. dxc5 Bxc5 7. Bf4 Bb7 8. e3 ! Nh5 {The point of the whole plan. Material is level, but only one side has a target to attack.} (8... Ba3 Rb1 Bxb2 {was the alternative — playable, but it hands over the initiative.}) 9. Bg3 Be7 10. Rd1 Nxg3 11. hxg3 Nc6 ?! 12. Be2 h6 13. Nb5 Qb8 14. O-O d6 15. Qa3 O-O 16. Nxd6 Rd8 17. c5 bxc5 18. Nxb7 Qxb7 19. Rb1 Qb6 20. Rfc1 Rac8 21. Qa4 Bf6 22. Qe4 Rd5 23. Bd3 g6 24. Bc4 Rdd8 25. g4 Kg7 26. Bf1 Rd5 27. Bc4 Rdd8 28. Be2 Rc7 29. g3 Nb4 30. a3 Nd5 31. Bf1 Qd6 32. Rd1 Rcd7 33. Nd2 Nb6 34. Qc2 Qe7 35. Be2 c4 36. Nxc4 Rxd1+ 37. Bxd1 Nxc4 38. Qxc4 Bxb2 39. Rxb2 Rxd1+ 40. Kg2 Qxa3 41. Rb7 Qa1 42. Qxe6 Rg1+ 43. Kh2 Rh1+ 44. Kg2 Rg1+ 45. Kh2 Rh1+ 46. Kg2 {Resignation is timely: the king cannot be defended and the counterplay is one move too slow.} Rg1+ 1/2-1/2
